== (1) the price sheet of a cycle shadow ==
 cycle, word, x_w, mu = 3^p/2^A, c_w = (p log_2 3 - A)/A bits of growth per bit of precision
  -1           (1,)                   x_w = -1     mu = 3/2        c_w = +0.58496
  -5           (1, 2)                 x_w = -5     mu = 9/8        c_w = +0.05664
  -17          (1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 4)  x_w = -17    mu = 2187/2048  c_w = +0.00861
  1 (trivial)  (2,)                   x_w = 1      mu = 3/4        c_w = -0.20752
 exactness U^(pr)(m) - x_w = mu^r (m - x_w) = 3^(pr) 2^(K - Ar) u, word = w^r, for K <= 60, u in {1,3,7}: True
 (cycle, K, periods r, growth in bits, c_w K, log_2(m + |x_w|)): the growth is at most c_w K and K <= log_2(m + |x_w|)
   ('-1', 24, 23, 13.454, 14.039, 24.0)
   ('-1', 60, 59, 34.513, 35.098, 60.0)
   ('-5', 24, 7, 1.189, 1.359, 24.0)
   ('-5', 60, 19, 3.229, 3.399, 60.0)
   ('-17', 24, 2, 0.189, 0.207, 24.0)
   ('-17', 60, 5, 0.474, 0.517, 60.0)
   ('1 (trivial)', 24, 11, -4.565, -4.98, 24.0)
   ('1 (trivial)', 60, 29, -12.036, -12.451, 60.0)
 reading: the -1 shadow (all ones) attains the universal maximum log_2 3 - 1 = 0.585 growth bits per bit of precision;
 the -5 and -17 shadows are cheap approaches (0.057 and 0.0086 bits per bit); the trivial cycle charges -0.2075: descent
== (2) the three-place multiplier identity along a shadow ==
 for same-word pairs over r periods: real distance x 3^(pr)/2^(Ar), 2-adic distance x 2^(Ar), 3-adic distance x 3^(-pr); product 1: True
 reading: the Syracuse map expands 2-adically by 2^v, contracts 3-adically by 1/3 (within a valuation class) and the real
 multiplier 3/2^v is the reciprocal of their product; real growth is exactly the excess of 3-adic contraction over 2-adic expansion
== (3) regeneration depths along orbits (n < 2*10^5) ==
 size bound v_2(m_j - x) <= log_2(m_j + |x|) violated: 0 times (a theorem: a positive integer = x mod 2^k with x < 0 is >= 2^k - |x|)
 x = -1: max depth 17 (at n = 77671); max (depth - log_2 n) = 1.25; P(depth >= 2,4,...,12) = [0.50029, 0.12416, 0.02672, 0.00396, 0.00095, 0.00021] vs the uniform law 2^(1-k) = [0.5, 0.125, 0.03125, 0.00781, 0.00195, 0.00049]
 x = -5: max depth 18 (at n = 174759); max (depth - log_2 n) = 3.25; P(depth >= 2,4,...,12) = [0.50029, 0.12905, 0.0273, 0.01178, 0.00082, 0.00012] vs the uniform law 2^(1-k) = [0.5, 0.125, 0.03125, 0.00781, 0.00195, 0.00049]
 x = -17: max depth 18 (at n = 174751); max (depth - log_2 n) = 2.51; P(depth >= 2,4,...,12) = [0.50029, 0.12416, 0.03034, 0.0049, 0.00091, 0.0002] vs the uniform law 2^(1-k) = [0.5, 0.125, 0.03125, 0.00781, 0.00195, 0.00049]
 reading: approaches to a negative cycle point deeper than log_2 n do occur (regenerated precision), but never deeper than log_2 of the current value
