 the rest is generic no-descent words, which no cycle label prices
== (5) explicit double-excursion integers: the reset lane's obstruction, priced ==
 cycle -5: word w^8 + (2, 2) + w^16: least source n has 75.1 bits (class mod 2^77)
   K_1 = 25, growth_1 = 1.36 bits; bridge dip = -0.83 bits; re-entry depth K_2 = 51 (> K_1: True), growth_2 = 2.72 bits; whole orbit above n: True
   ledger: dip 0.83 < growth_2 2.72, so log m + (prepaid growth) jumps up at the re-entry; source price c_w (K_1 + K_2) = 4.30 bits against log_2 n = 75.1; total growth 3.25 = 0.043 log_2 n
 cycle -5: word w^12 + (2, 2, 2) + w^24: least source n has 114.7 bits (class mod 2^115)
   K_1 = 37, growth_1 = 2.04 bits; bridge dip = -1.25 bits; re-entry depth K_2 = 74 (> K_1: True), growth_2 = 4.08 bits; whole orbit above n: True
   ledger: dip 1.25 < growth_2 4.08, so log m + (prepaid growth) jumps up at the re-entry; source price c_w (K_1 + K_2) = 6.29 bits against log_2 n = 114.7; total growth 4.87 = 0.042 log_2 n
 cycle -5: word w^16 + (2, 2, 2) + w^40: least source n has 174.3 bits (class mod 2^175)
   K_1 = 49, growth_1 = 2.72 bits; bridge dip = -1.25 bits; re-entry depth K_2 = 123 (> K_1: True), growth_2 = 6.80 bits; whole orbit above n: True
   ledger: dip 1.25 < growth_2 6.80, so log m + (prepaid growth) jumps up at the re-entry; source price c_w (K_1 + K_2) = 9.74 bits against log_2 n = 174.3; total growth 8.27 = 0.047 log_2 n
 cycle -1: word w^10 + (2, 2) + w^20: least source n has 34.5 bits (class mod 2^35)
   K_1 = 11, growth_1 = 5.85 bits; bridge dip = -0.83 bits; re-entry depth K_2 = 23 (> K_1: True), growth_2 = 11.70 bits; whole orbit above n: True
   ledger: dip 0.83 < growth_2 11.70, so log m + (prepaid growth) jumps up at the re-entry; source price c_w (K_1 + K_2) = 19.89 bits against log_2 n = 34.5; total growth 16.72 = 0.485 log_2 n
 cycle -1: word w^16 + (2, 2, 2) + w^32: least source n has 54.8 bits (class mod 2^55)
   K_1 = 17, growth_1 = 9.36 bits; bridge dip = -1.25 bits; re-entry depth K_2 = 34 (> K_1: True), growth_2 = 18.72 bits; whole orbit above n: True
   ledger: dip 1.25 < growth_2 18.72, so log m + (prepaid growth) jumps up at the re-entry; source price c_w (K_1 + K_2) = 29.83 bits against log_2 n = 54.8; total growth 26.83 = 0.490 log_2 n
 reading: the second approach is deeper than the first (regenerated precision), the dip between them is smaller than the
 second growth, and the whole orbit stays above the source: no bounded-lookahead potential is monotone on these; every such
 configuration is bought with a source of size 2^(precision consumed), so the combined growth is at most c_w times log_2 n
